WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:06.280
The second episode of Picard on CBS All Access
dropped a few truth-bombs about Captain Jean-Luc

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.849
Picard's health, some secret Romulan shenanigans,
and just exactly what happened during the

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:13.620
great synthetic revolt that shattered the
Federation.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.520
Here are a few things you may not have noticed,
between the lines.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.560
"Maps and Legends" begins on that fateful
day in 2385 when the synthetic workers stationed

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.480
at the Utopia Planitia shipyards on Mars rose
up against their flesh and blood coworkers,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.120
killing them and destroying the site so entirely
that it's still burning 14 years later.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.960
But before the day starts going very, very
badly, we get several shots of business as

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.240
usual in the shipyards, complete with a close-up
of what kind of looks like an Argo-type shuttlecraft.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.899
The Argo was first seen in Star Trek: Nemesis,
when Picard, Worf, and Data fly the shuttlecraft

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:51.999
from the Enterprise-E to the surface of the
planet Kolarus III, where they recover the

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:55.870
android B-4, who we've already seen in Picard's
first episode.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.100
The shuttlecraft calls back to an extremely
significant event in the lore of Picard, tying

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.460
into the show's central mystery of Data's
involvement in the creation of Dahj and Soji.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.580
April 5th is a very significant day in Star
Trek history.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.520
Established in 2063, it commemorates both
the first successful human warp flight, and

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.540
the first inter-species contact between humans
and Vulcans, as shown in Star Trek: First

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.040
Contact.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.240
First Contact Day has long been established
as an annual holiday in the world of Star

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:22.740
Trek.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.391
In Star Trek: Voyager, we even got to see
a First Contact Day celebration, complete

00:01:27.391 --> 00:01:31.770
with warp engine creator Zefram Cochrane's
favorite food, and the ship's Vulcan science

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.780
officer reciting the Vulcans' initial greeting
to the people of Earth.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.320
"Live long and prosper."

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.650
Captain Janeway also mentioned that as a child,
she always got the day off from school.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.900
In 2385, though, the workers at Utopia Planitia
complain that they still have to work on the

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.600
holiday, which they're not happy about.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.880
Having the attack on Mars take place on April
5th marks a sharp parallel to the day's original

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.670
theme of interspecies cooperation and trust.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.560
Following the attack, the Federation would
abandon their attempt to save the Romulan

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.220
people, and would also ban synthetics entirely.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.000
When Picard's doctor comes to visit him in
"Maps and Legends", he brings with him the

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.670
somber news that he suspects that a small
defect in Picard's parietal lobe may have

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.580
triggered one of several related neurological
syndromes.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.030
The two of them talk about the defect itself
as though the knowledge of it is old news,

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.819
which it is the defect was first discovered
by Dr. Beverly Crusher in the Next Generation

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.680
series finale, "All Good Things..."

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.380
That episode saw Picard skipping through time,
including one thread in the distant future,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.120
when he learns that he will eventually develop
Irumodic Syndrome, a degenerative neurological

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.460
condition that can cause confusion, hallucinations,
and paranoia.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.640
Picard has Crusher scan him in his "present"
timeline for possible warning signs, and while

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.560
she finds no traces of the syndrome, she does
notice a small defect in the parietal lobe

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.240
which could but won't necessarily eventually
lead to Irumodic Syndrome.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.460
While neither Picard nor his doctor mention
Irumodic Syndrome by name in "Maps and Legends,"

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.100
it's clear that this is what Picard is worried
about.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.750
In "All Good Things...," we see that the future,
older Picard is often second-guessed and discounted

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:10.010
due to his diagnosis, leading to him going
against the orders of Starfleet and calling

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:14.390
in favors to obtain a ship and carry out his
mission a plotline that seems like it may

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:16.080
be rearing its head once again.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.920
When Picard steps into the lobby of Starfleet
Headquarters, a voice can be heard over the

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.700
intercom paging a certain Admiral.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.700
"Admiral Gurney, please report to conference
room A."

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.900
Although it's easy to dismiss the line as
simple background chatter, the name "Gurney"

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.680
has some surprising significance.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.780
In the 1984 science fiction film Dune, Patrick
Stewart played a character named Gurney Halleck,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.060
a skilled musician and a formidable warrior.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.460
Although Gurney Halleck's specific character
traits are likely not particularly relevant

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:48.140
to Picard, the name over the pager is almost
assuredly a sly tip of the hat to Stewart's

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.390
previous character.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.760
Making the reference even more amusing?

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.380
"I never really cared for science fiction."

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.020
When Picard meets with Dr. Jurati, he comes
upon her paging through Isaac Asimov's The

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:05.220
Complete Robot, a collection of science fiction
short stories about, what else, robots.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.780
Asimov's fiction often explored ideas of sentience
and morality in robots, which was also one

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:15.000
of the main philosophical quandaries of Star
Trek: The Next Generation, and is shaping

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.020
up to be a central question of Picard.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.380
It feels additionally significant that The
Complete Robot makes an appearance in an episode

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.680
that opens with a scene of robots violently
acting out against their creators.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.300
All three of the actions shown by F-8 in the
opening sequence are expressly prohibited

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.639
by Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics: a robot
may not injure a human being or, through inaction,

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:41.280
allow a human being to come to harm; a robot
must obey the orders given it by human beings

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.160
except where such orders would conflict with
the First Law; and a robot must protect its

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.950
own existence as long as such protection does
not conflict with the First or Second Laws.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.380
Looks like F-8 didn't get the memo.

